<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    nexus-cbo4a batch 9 item 0 (Sam's directive, 2026-09-05, nexus-zrcj7): relocate
    the pgvector-family extensions (vector, pg_trgm) into the nexus schema, so the
    33 functions in search-path-002-qualify-vector-operator-functions.xml can
    reference nexus.vector / nexus.word_similarity / OPERATOR(nexus.&lt;=&gt;) /
    OPERATOR(nexus.&lt;%) explicitly instead of depending on the calling session's
    search_path containing public (where CREATE EXTENSION landed unqualified,
    vectors-001-baseline.xml).

    REDESIGNED (T2 nexus/critique-nexus-cbo4a-batch-9-search-path, ship-blocker):
    a FIRST version of this changeset performed the relocation itself
    (unconditional ALTER EXTENSION ... SET SCHEMA nexus), on the theory that an
    ownership-transfer step run by every extension-creation site (a throwaway
    superuser role, REASSIGN OWNED BY, DROP ROLE) would always hand nexus_admin
    ownership of both extensions before Liquibase ever connected. WRONG for every
    install that existed BEFORE this batch: those installs created vector/pg_trgm
    directly as the cluster's bootstrap superuser (oid 10, always - initdb's own
    role), the ownership-transfer backfill is a documented no-op for that specific
    case (REASSIGN OWNED BY unconditionally refuses to touch anything owned by the
    bootstrap superuser, by Postgres design, with no workaround), and nexus_admin
    - NOSUPERUSER, the only role that ever runs this changelog outside the
    superuser-driven test/jOOQ-codegen paths - can never itself execute ALTER
    EXTENSION on an extension it does not own. Every existing local install would
    fail this changeset on its very first walk past this batch and never boot.

    THE FIX: relocation is no longer performed here directly by nexus_admin, since
    nexus_admin structurally cannot own the extension or become superuser. Two
    mechanisms now do the actual work, for two different sequencing shapes:

    - PRE-EXISTING install, EVERY daemon start: nexus.db.pg_provision's
      relocate_vector_extensions_to_nexus_schema (direct=True) relocates directly,
      as the cluster's OS-level superuser (os_user), OUTSIDE Liquibase entirely and
      strictly BEFORE this changelog ever runs (storage_service_daemon._ensure_pg_
      running -> _backfill_provision_grants -> provision(), Step 1 of every
      _start_locked, before Step 2 spawns the service binary). Superuser bypasses
      the ownership check entirely, and this is always safe for a pre-existing
      install regardless of when it happens: vectors-001-baseline.xml's own bare
      vector(N)/vector_cosine_ops references (vectors-001-2/-3/-4) already ran to
      completion, in a PAST walk, years before this batch shipped - relocating the
      extension now changes nothing about their already-materialized column types.
      For a managed/production cluster, a documented DBA pre-step (SchemaMigrator's
      own Phase-5 provisioning javadoc) runs the same two ALTER EXTENSION
      statements as a superuser, once, before the first walk carrying this batch.
      conexus's PITR-fork walk rehearsal (the real pre-deploy gate for any tag
      carrying a changeset) exercises this changelog directly, so a skipped
      pre-step there is caught as the FAIL LOUD case below, never a silent no-op.

    - FRESH install, first-ever walk: the pre-existing-install fix above cannot
      apply here, because a from-scratch install has no PAST walk - it runs
      vectors-001-baseline.xml's bare references in the SAME continuous walk this
      changeset belongs to, and relocating the extension before THAT walk starts
      breaks those same vectors-001-2/-3/-4 changesets (nexus is not on
      nexus_admin's default, public-only search_path, so their bare vector(N)/
      vector_cosine_ops references fail to resolve once the extension is no longer
      in public). Those changesets cannot be edited to qualify them - every
      EXISTING install already applied that exact text and Liquibase checksums it,
      so editing it is the NO-EDIT-APPLIED-CHANGESETS hot rule violated for every
      one of them - and pinning a search_path on the migration connection just to
      keep them resolving bare would silently reintroduce, for the WHOLE
      changelog, the exact reliance this entire batch exists to retire. So instead:
      nexus.db.pg_provision installs a narrow SECURITY DEFINER function,
      nexus.ensure_vector_extensions_relocated() (owned by os_user, granted
      EXECUTE to nexus_admin), during a from-scratch provision (direct=False - see
      that function's own docstring, "FRESH-INSTALL SEQUENCING" section, for the
      full derivation). A SECURITY DEFINER function executes its body with the
      OWNER's privileges, not the caller's, so nexus_admin - though NOSUPERUSER -
      can call it, by its fully-qualified name (no search_path needed to resolve a
      qualified call), and have it relocate the extension AT EXACTLY THIS POINT in
      the SAME single continuous walk: late, well after vectors-001-2/-3/-4 already
      ran with the extension still resolvable in public. This changeset's own body
      (below) calls that function when it exists and the extensions are not
      already relocated.

    This changeset's OWN job, given nexus_admin can never relocate the extension by
    itself directly, is to GUARD that relocation has happened (or make it happen,
    via the SECURITY DEFINER function, when this is the one walk that can only be
    fixed here), with a THIRD tier, tried first, that keeps every superuser-
    driven walk (PgContainerHelper#applyProductSchema, the jOOQ codegen plugin's
    Testcontainers bootstrap) working with ZERO extra setup, exactly as before
    this redesign:

    - Precondition checks whether BOTH extensions already report
      extnamespace = 'nexus'. If so (the expected steady state - every walk after
      the pre-existing-install fast-path fix above has already run at least once,
      or a walk resuming after this changeset's own prior successful run), onFail=
      "MARK_RAN" fires - there is nothing left to do, so it is marked applied
      without executing its body.
    - If NOT - either extension is still in public - the precondition FAILS to
      match, so the changeset body runs, in this order:
      1. ATTEMPT the ALTER EXTENSION statements directly, inside a nested
         exception-handling block. This succeeds immediately, with no further
         action, whenever the CONNECTING role is superuser (every su-driven test
         walk and the jOOQ codegen plugin's own bootstrap) OR happens to already
         own both extensions - covering every shape this changeset covered
         before this SECURITY DEFINER addition, unchanged. A repeat ALTER
         EXTENSION to the SAME schema an extension is already in succeeds
         silently (verified empirically), so attempting both unconditionally,
         even if only one of the two actually needs to move, is harmless.
      2. On `insufficient_privilege` (SQLSTATE 42501 - Postgres's "must be owner
         of extension" error for this specific case), the nested block's
         exception handler fires: call nexus.ensure_vector_extensions_relocated()
         when that function exists (the fresh-install, NOSUPERUSER-migrating-role
         case - see above), which performs the relocation via SECURITY DEFINER
         elevation and returns normally.
      3. WHY A MISSING FUNCTION MUST HALT, NOT SKIP: search-path-002's 33
         functions already reference nexus.vector / OPERATOR(nexus.&lt;=&gt;)
         explicitly; a silent skip here would let those functions get CREATE OR
         REPLACEd against a name (nexus.vector) that does not exist yet, which
         fails LATER, on a DIFFERENT changeset, with a raw "schema nexus does not
         exist" or "type nexus.vector does not exist" error that names no remedy
         at all - strictly worse than failing HERE, on the changeset whose entire
         job is checking exactly this precondition, with a message naming the
         exact fix. A missing function here (after tier 1 already failed with
         insufficient_privilege) means the pg_provision.py path never ran (a
         managed/production cluster where the DBA forgot the documented pre-step
         and never installed the function either) - the only remaining case, so
         the changeset RAISEs an exception naming the two ALTER EXTENSION
         statements a superuser needs to run. None of these three tiers require
         ownership of anything to ATTEMPT - a PL/pgSQL DO block executes as the
         calling role regardless of what it does or does not own, and a failed
         attempt, a qualified function call (permissions checked at call time via
         GRANT EXECUTE, not ownership), and RAISE EXCEPTION are all ordinary
         operations any role can attempt.

    RECOVERY FROM A HALT BETWEEN THIS CHANGESET AND search-path-002: this
    changeset itself makes NO schema change when it halts (the RAISE EXCEPTION
    branch runs before any DDL statement), so a walk that halts there has changed
    NOTHING relative to the walk's state one changeset earlier - there is no
    half-applied state to reason about for the HALT case specifically. (The
    SUCCESS case, calling the SECURITY DEFINER function, DOES make a schema
    change - exactly the intended one, and the function is itself idempotent, so a
    resumed walk re-entering this changeset after an interruption elsewhere finds
    the precondition already satisfied and MARK_RANs instead.) The fix for a halt
    is exactly the message names: run the two ALTER EXTENSION statements as a
    superuser (or, for a local install, simply start the daemon again once its own
    relocate step has had a chance to run - the halt here means it did not run, or
    ran and failed, before Liquibase started), then re-run the walk. Liquibase's
    own retry-from-the-first-unapplied-changeset behavior picks this changeset back
    up cleanly since it was never marked applied; both this changeset and
    search-path-002's 33 (each independently idempotent via CREATE OR REPLACE)
    tolerate a re-run with no cleanup step required.

    ROLLBACK: NOT a no-op, and this is deliberate. This changeset's rollback moves
    both extensions back to public via the identical three-tier logic as the
    forward direction, in reverse (direct ALTER first, falling back on
    insufficient_privilege to a companion SECURITY DEFINER function,
    nexus.ensure_vector_extensions_unrelocated(), installed by nexus.db.pg_provision
    alongside the forward one). WHY THIS CANNOT BE A NO-OP, discovered live by
    SchemaRollbackRoundTripIntegrationTest's rollback-to-zero scenario: search-
    path-002's 33 rollbacks (which fire FIRST, being applied LAST) restore their
    OWN nexus.*-qualified bodies, correctly, since the extension is still in nexus
    at that point - but every changeset OLDER than this one (rolling back NEXT, in
    reverse order) was written assuming vector resolves BARE via public, exactly
    like vectors-001-baseline.xml's own forward-direction changesets. A no-op
    rollback here would leave vector permanently in nexus for the rest of a
    rollback-to-zero walk, failing the very next older changeset that references
    it bare with a raw "type vector does not exist" error - the identical
    sequencing constraint the forward direction has, mirrored. Rollback-to-zero is
    a test-only path (production never rolls back past its own installed version),
    so unlike the forward direction there is no separate DBA-runbook concern here;
    the same fail-loud RAISE EXCEPTION tier still applies for diagnosability if
    genuinely neither the direct attempt nor the function succeeds.
-->
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.4.xsd">

    <changeSet id="search-path-001-1" author="nexus-cbo4a">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <sqlCheck expectedResult="false">
SELECT COALESCE(
    (
        (SELECT extnamespace::regnamespace::text FROM pg_extension WHERE extname = 'vector') = 'nexus'
        AND (SELECT extnamespace::regnamespace::text FROM pg_extension WHERE extname = 'pg_trgm') = 'nexus'
    ),
    false
)::text
            </sqlCheck>
        </preConditions>
        <comment>Guard that vector and pg_trgm are already relocated into the nexus schema before search-path-002's 33 functions reference nexus.vector / OPERATOR(nexus.&lt;=&gt;). Skips (MARK_RAN) when both are already relocated. Otherwise: attempts the ALTER EXTENSION statements directly (succeeds when the connecting role is superuser or already owns both, e.g. every su-driven test/jOOQ-codegen walk, unchanged from before this redesign); on insufficient_privilege, calls the SECURITY DEFINER nexus.ensure_vector_extensions_relocated() when it exists (the fresh-install, NOSUPERUSER-migrating-role case); halts with the exact remedy when that function is also absent (a managed/production cluster whose DBA pre-step never ran).</comment>
        <sql splitStatements="false" stripComments="false">
DO $$
BEGIN
    BEGIN
        EXECUTE 'ALTER EXTENSION vector SET SCHEMA nexus';
        EXECUTE 'ALTER EXTENSION pg_trgm SET SCHEMA nexus';
    EXCEPTION WHEN insufficient_privilege THEN
        IF to_regprocedure('nexus.ensure_vector_extensions_relocated()') IS NOT NULL THEN
            PERFORM nexus.ensure_vector_extensions_relocated();
        ELSE
            RAISE EXCEPTION 'nexus-cbo4a batch 9: vector/pg_trgm are not yet relocated into the nexus schema, this role cannot relocate them directly, and no nexus.ensure_vector_extensions_relocated() helper is installed to do it from here. This must happen as a superuser step BEFORE this Liquibase walk. For a local install, start the nx daemon again (nexus.db.pg_provision relocates on every daemon start, before this walk ever runs). Otherwise, run as a superuser: ALTER EXTENSION vector SET SCHEMA nexus; ALTER EXTENSION pg_trgm SET SCHEMA nexus;';
        END IF;
    END;
END $$;
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql splitStatements="false" stripComments="false">
DO $$
BEGIN
    BEGIN
        EXECUTE 'ALTER EXTENSION vector SET SCHEMA public';
        EXECUTE 'ALTER EXTENSION pg_trgm SET SCHEMA public';
    EXCEPTION WHEN insufficient_privilege THEN
        IF to_regprocedure('nexus.ensure_vector_extensions_unrelocated()') IS NOT NULL THEN
            PERFORM nexus.ensure_vector_extensions_unrelocated();
        ELSE
            RAISE EXCEPTION 'nexus-cbo4a batch 9 rollback: vector/pg_trgm could not be moved back to public, this role cannot relocate them directly, and no nexus.ensure_vector_extensions_unrelocated() helper is installed to do it from here. Run as a superuser: ALTER EXTENSION vector SET SCHEMA public; ALTER EXTENSION pg_trgm SET SCHEMA public;';
        END IF;
    END;
END $$;
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
